> Why this ratio permits reuse

Let ρ=(√5−1)/2 and W=b−a. The symmetric probes are c=b−ρW
and d=a+ρW. If f(c)<f(d), strict increase up to a single peak and strict decrease
after it exclude any maximum to the left of c. Retain [c,b]. If f(c)>f(d),
retain [a,d]. Under those same strict assumptions, equal values straddle the
peak; the native tie rule retains [a,d], which still contains it in exact arithmetic.
This implementation uses that one-sided tie rule and never evaluates endpoints.
It does not check the assumptions.

For the retained interval [c,b], the new left probe must equal old d.
Substituting the new width ρW yields

$$
b-\rho^2 W=a+\rho W,\qquad \rho^2+\rho=1,\qquad
\rho=\frac{\sqrt5-1}{2}.
$$
\alttext(Equating the reused probe positions gives rho squared plus rho equals one. The positive root is square root of five minus one, divided by two.)

The other branch is symmetric. Thus, in real arithmetic,

$$
W_t=\rho^t W_0,\qquad
|x_t-x^*|\leq W_t/2\ \text{if }x^*\in[a_t,b_t],\qquad
N_f=t+3.
$$
\alttext(After t updates the ideal width is rho to the power t times its initial width. A midpoint has error at most half the width only if the true peak remains inside. Actual callback calls equal t plus three.)

For 0<ε<W0, the ideal required number of updates is the ceiling of
log(W0/ε)/log(1/ρ). This is linear convergence of interval width, not a
promise of that many correct decimal digits in the returned location. With
fixed-cost evaluations the selected work is O(t), and ordinary solver storage
is constant. Expensive or stateful objectives have their own costs.

> Follow the original sine example without replaying its search

The original objective is sin(x)−0.1(x−1.5)² on [0,3]. Its second derivative
is −sin(x)−0.2<0 there, so its unique maximum solves cos(x)−0.2(x−1.5)=0.
An independent derivative bisection gives approximately 1.5589967108207259;
that reference is used below. The native solver needs 51 updates at width
tolerance 1e−10, but its returned location differs from this root by more than
1e−8. The small bracket no longer contains the mathematical maximizer after
rounded values cease to distinguish the probes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/golden_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/golden_section_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 auto f=[](double x){return std::sin(x)-.1*(x-1.5)*(x-1.5);};
 const auto t=dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,0,3,1e-10);
 constexpr double reference=1.5589967108207259;
 const double rho=(std::sqrt(5.)-1)/2;
 std::vector<double> iteration,actual,ideal;
 std::print("| update | actual width | ideal real width | actual calls so far |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto& e:t.events)if(e.kind!=dm::algorithms::GoldenEventKind::stop){
  const double w=e.after.b-e.after.a,predicted=3*std::pow(rho,e.iteration);
  iteration.push_back(e.iteration);actual.push_back(std::log10(w));ideal.push_back(std::log10(predicted));
  if(e.iteration%5==0||e.iteration==t.run.result.iterations)std::print("| {} | {:.12e} | {:.12e} | {} |\n",e.iteration,w,predicted,e.evaluations);
 }
 const auto& q=t.run.final_state;
 std::print("\nReturned x {:.17g}, value {:.17g}; updates {}; total actual calls {}; retained records {}; stop {}. Independent reference {:.17g}; absolute error {:.12e}; final half-width {:.12e}; reference inside final bracket {}.\n",t.run.result.argmax,t.run.result.value,t.run.result.iterations,t.run.evaluations,t.events.size(),dm::algorithms::golden_stop_name(t.run.stop),reference,std::abs(t.run.result.argmax-reference),(q.b-q.a)/2,q.a<=reference&&reference<=q.b);
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(iteration,ideal,"ideal",{5,150,105},3).line(iteration,actual,"actual",{37,99,235},1.5)
  .legend({"Ideal real width","Actual native width"},{{5,150,105},{37,99,235}}).title("Actual width follows the geometric scale, with rounding")
  .xlab("Completed updates").ylab("log10(bracket width)").x_limits(0,51).y_limits(-11,2.5).save_svg("build/golden_original.svg");
 if(t.run.result.iterations!=51||t.run.evaluations!=54||q.a<=reference&&reference<=q.b)throw std::runtime_error("original numerical witness");

}
