> Flat tails and floating endpoints are different failure mechanisms

A triangular bump max(0,1−20 abs(x−0.9)) has one peak, but its flat nonmaximum
tails violate the strict monotonicity used in the comparison proof. The first
two probes both see zero, so the left tie rule discards the true peak. A tiny
returned bracket cannot repair that loss. Merely saying “rises then falls” is
therefore too weak when it includes flat tails.

At adjacent representable endpoints, new probes can coincide with old points.
The solver preserves its original behavior: it reaches the requested iteration
limit rather than silently applying a different stagnation rule. Noise and
multiple peaks also invalidate the deterministic comparison argument; no
robust noisy-optimization claim is made here.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/golden_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/golden_section_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 auto bump=[](double x){return std::max(0.,1-20*std::abs(x-.9));};
 const double next=std::nextafter(1.,2.);
 std::vector<dm::algorithms::GoldenRun> runs{dm::algorithms::golden_section_run(bump,0,1,1e-10),dm::algorithms::golden_section_run([](double x){return x;},1,next,1e-30),dm::algorithms::golden_section_run([](double x){return -(x-.9)*(x-.9);},0,1,1e-10,1),dm::algorithms::golden_section_run([](double x){return x;},0,1,1,0)};
 const std::vector<std::string> labels{"Flat","Adjacent","Cap one","Short"};const std::vector<double> truth{.9,next,.9,1};std::vector<double> updates;
 std::print("| case | returned x | value | final width | updates | calls | true peak inside | stop |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n");
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<runs.size();++i){const auto& r=runs[i];updates.push_back(r.result.iterations);std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.12g} | {:.12e} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",labels[i],r.result.argmax,r.result.value,r.final_state.b-r.final_state.a,r.result.iterations,r.evaluations,r.final_state.a<=truth[i]&&truth[i]<=r.final_state.b,dm::algorithms::golden_stop_name(r.stop));}
 std::vector<double> x,y;for(int i=0;i<=200;++i){x.push_back(i/200.);y.push_back(bump(x.back()));}
 auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.line(x,y,"bump",{5,150,105},2).points({runs[0].result.argmax},{runs[0].result.value},"returned",{220,38,38},6)
  .legend({"Objective bump","Returned midpoint"},{{5,150,105},{220,38,38}}).title("Flat tails hide the peak from both probes").xlab("x").ylab("Objective").x_limits(0,1).y_limits(0,1.5);
 auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(updates,labels);b.title("Actual completed updates").xlab("").ylab("Bracket updates").y_limits(0,600);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,500).title("A small width and an iteration cap report different facts").fit_panels().save_svg("build/golden_limits.svg");
 if(runs[0].result.value!=0||runs[1].result.iterations!=500||runs[1].evaluations!=503)throw std::runtime_error("preserved limitations");

}
