> Bound complete records without replay or hidden callback work

A trace owns H=t+2 complete initial/update/stop records. The default optional
quota is one million records; zero disables it while retaining the whole history.
Each next record is admitted before its associated callback work. A one-update
trace needs three records, including its final stop. Rejecting a cap of two
occurs after the two initial and one step callbacks, but before the midpoint
callback. Already performed callback side effects cannot be rolled back.

Root/input/callable/ordinary result/final state/constant working storage and
allocator overhead are excluded. Records are not numeric entries, bytes or peak
RAM. Copying a trace owns independent scalar records; it does not clone external
state referenced by the original callable. No callback is retained in the trace.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/golden_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/golden_section_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 int calls=0;auto f=[&](double x){++calls;return x;};bool rejected=false;
 try{(void)dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,0,1,0,1,2);}catch(const std::length_error&){rejected=true;}
 const int rejected_calls=calls;calls=0;auto t=dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,0,1,0,1,3);const int admitted_calls=calls;
 auto copy=t;copy.events[0].after.a=99;const bool owned=t.events[0].after.a==0;
 calls=0;bool empty_rejected=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,0,1,1,0,1);}catch(const std::length_error&){empty_rejected=true;}const int no_step_calls=calls;
 calls=0;bool invalid=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,2,1,0,1,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){invalid=true;}const int invalid_calls=calls;
 const auto unlimited=dm::algorithms::golden_section_trace(f,0,1,0,3,0);
 bool bad=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::golden_section_search([](double){return std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();},0,1);}catch(const std::domain_error&){bad=true;}
 std::print("| admission | required records | rejected quota | rejected | calls before rejection |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n| One complete update plus metadata | {} | 2 | {} | {} |\n| No-update initial and stop | 2 | 1 | {} | {} |\n",t.events.size(),rejected,rejected_calls,empty_rejected,no_step_calls);
 std::print("\nAdmitted one-update trace makes {} calls. Zero optional quota retains {} complete records. Independent owned copy {}; invalid endpoints rejected {} before {} calls; nonfinite objective rejected {}.\n",admitted_calls,unlimited.events.size(),owned,invalid,invalid_calls,bad);
 auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(t.events.size()),2.},{"Required","Rejected cap"});a.title("Complete initial, step and stop records").xlab("").ylab("Whole records").y_limits(0,4);
 auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(rejected_calls),double(admitted_calls)},{"Rejected","Admitted"});b.title("Earlier callback side effects remain").xlab("").ylab("Actual calls").y_limits(0,5);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,500).title("The final stop record and midpoint call count too").fit_panels().save_svg("build/golden_controls.svg");
 if(!rejected||!empty_rejected||!owned||!invalid||invalid_calls||!bad)throw std::runtime_error("record admission controls");

}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| admission | required records | rejected quota | rejected | calls before rejection |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| One complete update plus metadata | 3 | 2 | true | 3 |
| No-update initial and stop | 2 | 1 | true | 2 |

Admitted one-update trace makes 4 calls. Zero optional quota retains 5 complete records. Independent owned copy true; invalid endpoints rejected true before 0 calls; nonfinite objective rejected true.
\image(figures/golden_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Record capacity and actual callbacks have different units. A rejected trace returns no partial result, although its earlier callback side effects remain observable. Input parameter validation precedes record admission.)
\alttext(One panel compares three required whole records with a rejected cap of two. The other compares three callbacks before rejection with four callbacks on admission. The table also retains a no-update two-record rejection and its two initial callbacks.)

Nonfinite endpoints/tolerance, reversed intervals and negative update limits are
rejected. Absolute tolerance may be zero; equal endpoints are valid and still
make three calls. Evaluated nonfinite coordinate arithmetic or objective values
throw rather than propagate invalid results. The legacy midpoint expression
0.5(a+b) is preserved, so its sum can overflow even when both endpoints are finite;
that case is rejected rather than silently switching formulas.

> What this analysis establishes

The shared core now supplies the actual bracket history, so report code presents
rather than reconstructs the search. Probe reuse gives one new call per update,
with two initialization calls and one final midpoint call. Under strict
unimodality and faithful comparisons the midpoint error is bounded by half the
retained width. The observed floating-point and flat-tail counterexamples show
why that condition belongs beside the bound. These controlled objectives expose
specific mechanisms; an unrelated dataset would not test the solver assumptions.

> References and implementation scope

Dimitri P. Bertsekas, *Nonlinear Programming*, Third Edition, Appendix C.3,
pp.812–814, presents strict unimodality and reusable golden probes:
[author-hosted appendix](https://web.mit.edu/dimitrib/www/NLP_Appendixes_Refs.pdf).
That minimizer includes endpoint checks and a different tie contraction; the
native maximizer preserves its original strict branch and one-sided tie rule.
The derivation and numerical checks above describe this implementation.

J. Kiefer (1953), “Sequential Minimax Search for a Maximum”, *Proceedings of the
American Mathematical Society* 4(3),502–506,
[DOI](https://doi.org/10.2307/2032161), gives the historical sequential minimax
context. Its bibliography was verified; the full article was inaccessible and
is not claimed as a read implementation source. No finite-budget minimax
optimality claim is made for this fixed-ratio native routine.
